    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Prague grew from a castle of the Přemyslid rulers into the seat of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and under Charles IV, who was crowned emperor in 1355, it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</li><li>The city was the centre of the Hussite movement after the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a> in 1415, and the defenestration of 1618 and the defeat at White Mountain in 1620 began its re-Catholicisation and a long decline.</li><li>Prague became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1918, was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1939 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> in 1968, and has been the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> since 1993.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The city's four boroughs of Hradčany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mala-strana">Malá Strana</a>, Staré Město and Nové Město were merged into a single entity in 1784 under Joseph II, and the Jewish quarter of Josefov was added in 1850.</p></aside>
    </section>

